Q: How do you increase API performance?

Interview structure: measure first → fix hottest bottleneck (DB / app / network / scale).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 1. Code / API design

- Return DTOs (only needed fields), avoid fat entities / SELECT *
- Fix N+1 (JOIN FETCH / EntityGraph / DTO projection)
- Async / messaging for non-critical work (email, notifications) → Kafka/RabbitMQ/@Async
- Keep APIs stateless for easy horizontal scale
- Pagination for lists (Page/Slice/cursor for huge data)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 2. Database (usually #1 bottleneck)

- Indexes on WHERE / JOIN / ORDER BY columns (verify with EXPLAIN)
- Connection pool: HikariCP tuned (not blindly huge)
- Avoid chatty ORM; batch where useful
- Cache hot reads
- Read replicas for heavy SELECTs
- Partition/shard only when truly needed

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 3. Caching

Redis / Caffeine for:
- repeated DB reads
- configs / reference data
- sessions / tokens (as designed)

@Cacheable("users")
public User getUserById(Long id) { ... }

Always define TTL/eviction; cache stampede awareness for hot keys.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 4. Network / payload

- GZIP/compression (Spring):
server.compression.enabled=true
server.compression.mime-types=application/json,application/xml,text/html,text/plain
server.compression.min-response-size=1024

- Smaller JSON (no unused fields)
- HTTP/2 for multiplexing + header compression (often with TLS)
server.http2.enabled=true

- gRPC (HTTP/2 + Protobuf) for internal service-to-service when low latency matters
  REST/HTTP still better for public/browser APIs

|          | Public/Browser | Internal microservices      |
| -------- | -------------- | --------------------------- |
| REST+HTTP/2 | ✅            | ✅ OK                       |
| gRPC        | ❌ usually    | ✅ often better             |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 5. Scale out

- Multiple app instances behind Gateway / NGINX / K8s Ingress
- Load balance round-robin / least-conn
- Rate limiting to protect capacity
- Scale read path (cache + replicas) before blind vertical scale

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 6. Measure

- Actuator + Micrometer → Prometheus/Grafana
- Slow query log / EXPLAIN
- APM / profiler (VisualVM, JProfiler, etc.)
Don’t optimize without metrics.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Spoken example

“User details API: first hit DB, then Redis cache; if traffic grows run N instances behind Nginx; if reads overload DB add a read replica; compress JSON and keep payloads small; fix N+1 and indexes first.”

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“I profile first, then optimize DB access and N+1, add caching, shrink payloads/compression, and scale horizontally with stateless services — using HTTP/2 or gRPC where it fits.”
